LUA(1)                         P32W Unix                          LUA(1)

NAME
       lua - Lua interpreter

SYNOPSIS
       lua [options] [script [args]]

DESCRIPTION
       lua is the stand-alone interpreter of Lua 5.4, the
       programming language from PUC-Rio (www.lua.org). It runs a
       script (a source file, or a binary chunk made by luac), with
       the arguments after it in the global table arg: the script's
       name in arg[0], its arguments in arg[1], arg[2] ..., the
       interpreter and the options before the script at negative
       indices. The arguments are also passed to the script as its
       ... (vararg).

       With no script and no -e, -v: an interactive session when
       the standard input is a terminal, else the standard input is
       run as a script (as lua -). In the interactive session (the
       read-eval-print loop) lua prompts with "> ", or ">> " while
       a statement is incomplete, and prints the values of an
       expression typed alone (also of = expr). The line is the
       terminal's own (backspace, ^U); there is no history. ^C stops
       a running chunk; ^D or os.exit() leaves.

       Before anything else lua runs LUA_INIT_5_4, or else LUA_INIT,
       from the environment: @file runs that file, anything else is
       Lua code (unless -E).

OPTIONS
       -e stat
              run the statement stat
       -i     enter interactive mode after the script
       -l mod
              require mod, as the global mod; -l g=mod: as the
              global g
       -v     print the version
       -E     ignore LUA_INIT and the LUA_PATH variables
       -W     turn warnings on
       --     stop handling options
       -      run the standard input as a script, and stop handling
              options

       Options are handled in order, so lua -e 'x = 1' script sees
       x.

NOTES
       Integers are 32 bits (math.maxinteger is 2147483647; integer
       arithmetic wraps around) and floats are IEEE doubles, in
       software, correctly rounded (+ - * / and sqrt), so they give
       the same results as on any IEEE machine. Numbers print as C's
       %.14g, as on other machines: 0.1, 0.33333333333333, 1e+100,
       2^53 as 9.007199254741e+15, -0.0, inf, -inf and nan.
       string.format has %d %i %u %c %x %X %o %e %E %f %F %g %G %a
       %A %q %s %p; string.pack's "f" is IEEE single precision.

       The whole standard library is there: string (patterns,
       format, pack and unpack), table, math, io (files, popen,
       tmpfile), os (time and date, execute, getenv, remove, rename,
       tmpname, clock, exit), utf8, coroutine, debug and package.
       require looks in /usr/lib/lua/5.4 and the current directory
       (package.path); there are no C modules. os.setlocale knows
       only "C"; os.clock counts the process's CPU time in 1/50 s.

EXAMPLES
       lua -e 'print(2^0.5, 7 // 2, 1 << 31)'
              1.4142135623731  3  -2147483648
       echo 'print(#arg, ...)' > a.lua; lua a.lua x y
              2  x  y
       lua    then, at the > prompt: 1 + 1

ENVIRONMENT
       LUA_INIT_5_4, LUA_INIT
              code run first (@file: a file)
       LUA_PATH_5_4, LUA_PATH
              package.path (;; in it stands for the default:
              /usr/lib/lua/5.4/?.lua;/usr/lib/lua/5.4/?/init.lua;
              ./?.lua;./?/init.lua)

FILES
       /usr/lib/lua/5.4
              Lua modules for require

EXIT STATUS
       0, or 1 after an error; os.exit(n) exits with n.

SEE ALSO
       luac(1), sh(1), awk(1), "Lua 5.4 Reference Manual" and
       "Programming in Lua" (www.lua.org). Lua is by Roberto
       Ierusalimschy, Waldemar Celes and Luiz Henrique de Figueiredo,
       Lua.org, PUC-Rio; MIT licence (/usr/src/lua/README.P32W).

BUGS
       A process has 8 MiB of address space, and the machine 8 MiB
       of RAM for all its processes: Lua and its data fit in about
       7 MB on a quiet machine. A table's array part (12 bytes an
       element) stops at a few hundred thousand elements, a string
       at a few megabytes; a recursion
       stops with "stack overflow" at 16,000 stack slots (some
       5,000 to 15,000 calls deep).

       It is slow: P32W has no floating-point hardware (a float
       operation is a library call of about 1,000 instructions) and
       the compiler does not optimise, so a Lua function call takes
       about 1 ms and a simple loop step 0.4 ms (emulator time;
       fib(25) takes about 4 minutes). Starting lua takes about 2 s.

       There are no C modules (no dynamic linking), no readline
       history, and no locales. %a with a precision rounds to
       nearest (the Mac's libc rounds its ties down). String hashing
       is seeded from the time, so the order of pairs() over string
       keys may change from run to run, as on other machines.
